# this file must contain only IPv4 addresses to be whitelisted (one per line)
# usage:
#   ./import-ip-blocklists.sh --whitelist-file ip-to-ban-whitelist.txt
# Note:
# - Always check policy/license and impact on false positives before using in production.
# - These sources are public and commonly used in anti-abuse contexts.
# Example of IPs to exclude from ban (IPs of legitimate services, providers, etc.)
# Example:
# 192.168.0.1
# 10.0.0.1
# 127.0.0.1 # localhost, always safe to whitelist
# 127.0.0.0/8 # localhost range, always safe to whitelist
# 10.0.0.0/8 # private network range, safe to whitelist
# 192.168.0.0/16 # private network range, safe to whitelist
# 172.16.0.0/12 # private network range, safe to whitelist
127.0.0.1
127.0.0.0/8
10.0.0.0/8
192.168.0.0/16
172.16.0.0/12




